    "B": 0.0,
    "O": 0.0,
    "source": "1810 and 1820 census interpolated to 1816, Black adults in Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina, Georgia, Tennessee and Louisiana; the census gives free people of colour without age or sex before 1820, so adults are estimated. The great majority were enslaved and could not vote. Free Black men meeting the property tests could legally vote only in North Carolina (until its 1835 constitution) and Tennessee (until its 1834 constitution); the other Southern states limited the vote to white men. In South Carolina, Georgia and Louisiana the legislature chose the electors, so no one there voted for president directly."
   }
